---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
export const prerender = false;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Measurement choices"
  description="What Woodhouse measures, what stays private and how to turn off anonymous activity reporting."
  path="/activity/measurement/"
  currentPath="/activity/"
>
  <article class="container measurement-page">
    <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/activity/">Activity</a>
      <span>/</span>
      <span>Measurement choices</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="page-title">A count. Not a profile.</h1>
    <p>
      Reviewed 16 October 2026. Woodhouse receives anonymous tab-session events from this site,
      Protocol 11 and SHOALSHOT. Other projects publish their own aggregate reports and keep their
      own privacy choices.
    </p>
    <h2>What is counted</h2>
    <p>
      Visits, active sessions, deliberate project-link clicks and reported gameplay starts,
      finishes, replays and active seconds. Active time requires a visible, focused, unpaused
      product with recent input. Music listening is reported separately by LoftwahFM.
    </p>
    <p>
      The report’s measure selector separates gameplay, visits, mini-app actions and audible
      listening. Open a project’s source details for its exact definitions and available fields.
      Starts and completions are not a matched cohort; dividing them does not establish a completion
      rate. Browser identifiers, where a project offers optional consent, are not unique people.
    </p>
    <h2>Dates, gaps and comparisons</h2>
    <p>
      Custom reports accept inclusive UTC dates, with up to 90 days in one window and no future
      dates. A closed calendar window does not guarantee complete measurement. Today, the first
      collection day, unavailable sources and expired history remain distinct. Missing values are
      gaps, never zeroes. The report freezes the selected dates, figures and capture time in its
      permanent link and exports.
    </p>
    <p>
      Historical production verification may be mixed into older counts and cannot always be
      identified or subtracted. The coverage calendar labels this legacy history. Growth findings
      require a source-declared comparison boundary, exact daily counts and complete adjacent UTC
      weeks. Bounded or sampled counters do not earn growth records. A movement in reports does not
      establish its cause.
    </p>
    <p>
      History limits belong to each source: App Composer retains a bounded 35-day activity window;
      LoftwahFM retains accepted listening aggregates for 185 days. Woodhouse keeps anonymous raw
      events for 90 days and durable daily aggregates beyond that. Game-owned distinct-browser
      measures may need raw history that has already expired even when daily counters remain. The
      source details in each dated report expose its actual coverage and limitations.
    </p>
    <h2>What this cannot tell you</h2>
    <p>
      This dashboard does not currently establish portfolio cohort retention, revenue, paying
      customers, acquisition cost, customer value or product–market fit. It does not join identities
      across products or record a person’s browsing journey. Privacy choices, offline use and
      blocked requests can hide real activity; accepted browser events can also be fabricated. Treat
      this as product evidence with stated limits, not an audited business statement.
    </p>
    <h2>What stays private</h2>
    <p>
      These three sources create a random tab-session identifier. It expires after 30 minutes of
      inactivity and does not identify a returning browser. We store a one-way hash, event IDs and
      timestamps with a 90-day retention window to deduplicate reports. Expired records are removed
      during collection or the hourly reporting refresh. A short-lived hash of the IP address limits
      request volume; the address itself is never stored. No email, account ID, save, precise
      location, page URL or raw referrer is collected by this collector.
    </p>
    <p>
      Daily totals and saved public reports contain aggregate numbers and may be kept indefinitely.
      Raw sessions and events never appear in public reports. Counts describe accepted reports, not
      verified humans. Offline play, network failures, browser blocking and privacy choices can
      cause undercounting.
    </p>
    <h2>Your choice</h2>
    <p>
      Do Not Track, Global Privacy Control, preview and designated test traffic are excluded. Add{" "}
      <code>?analytics=off</code> to a page address to disable this measurement for that visit.
      Anonymous play and saved progress work independently of reporting.
    </p>
    <p>
      <button type="button" data-analytics-choice>
        Turn off Woodhouse reporting on this browser
      </button>{" "}
      <span role="status" data-analytics-status></span>
    </p>
    <p>
      This browser setting applies to Woodhouse. Protocol 11 and SHOALSHOT honour their own{" "}
      <code>?analytics=off</code> option; Bubbles provides an explicit opt-in in its settings. There
      is no shared account or cross-project browser identity.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/activity/">Return to the public reports</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
<script>
  const button = document.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-analytics-choice]");
  const status = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-analytics-status]");
  const label = () => {
    try {
      const off = localStorage.getItem("woodhouse:analytics-opt-out") === "1";
      if (button)
        button.textContent = off
          ? "Turn on anonymous Woodhouse reporting"
          : "Turn off Woodhouse reporting on this browser";
      return off;
    } catch {
      if (status)
        status.textContent = "Browser storage is unavailable. Use ?analytics=off in the address.";
      return null;
    }
  };
  label();
  button?.addEventListener("click", () => {
    const off = label();
    if (off === null) return;
    // Revocation takes effect before persistence, including browsers that reject writes.
    if (!off) window.dispatchEvent(new Event("woodhouse:analytics-disable"));
    try {
      localStorage.setItem("woodhouse:analytics-opt-out", off ? "0" : "1");
      location.reload();
    } catch {
      if (status)
        status.textContent = off
          ? "Reporting remains off. This browser could not save the new choice."
          : "Reporting is off on this page. This browser could not save the choice for future pages. Use ?analytics=off in the address or try again.";
    }
  });
</script>
<style>
  .measurement-page {
    max-width: 76ch;
    padding-block: 2rem 5rem;
  }
  .measurement-page h2 {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
  }
  .measurement-page button {
    padding: 0.8rem 1rem;
    background: var(--acid);
    color: var(--ink);
    border: 0;
    font: inherit;
    cursor: pointer;
    min-height: 44px;
  }
</style>
